WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
Agent evaluation.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:06.074
An agent can get the right answer by a wasteful or lucky path.

3
00:00:06.074 --> 00:00:08.867
Here's how to catch that before production does.

4
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:12.467
A chatbot evaluation grades the answer.

5
00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:17.167
Agent evaluation grades the trajectory, the whole sequence of decisions.

6
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.633
Because many decisions happen on the way.

7
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:23.767
A right answer reached by a bad path will betray you in production.

8
00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:26.500
There are three core metrics.

9
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.167
Tool call accuracy: did the agent pick the right tool with the right arguments at each step?

10
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:36.567
Step efficiency: how many steps did it take, versus the minimal path?

11
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:40.700
And task completion: did the final state satisfy the rubric?

12
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.033
Here's the workflow.

13
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:47.883
First, hand label a small suite: twenty tasks with the expected tool calls.

14
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:49.400
That's your ground truth.

15
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.300
Run the agent and log every trajectory.

16
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.733
Grade each step.

17
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.312
Then aggregate into three numbers.

18
00:00:56.312 --> 00:01:00.133
An LLM judge comes later, calibrated against your labels.

19
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:03.067
Here is the loop, simplified.

20
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:07.200
For each labeled task, run the agent and log its trajectory.

21
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.300
For every tool call, we check whether it matches an expected call, both the tool and its arguments.

22
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.200
We also grade completion against the rubric and count the steps.

23
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.800
Now a real result from a twenty task suite.

24
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.281
The first run: completion eighteen out of twenty, which looks great.

25
00:01:25.281 --> 00:01:32.167
But tool call accuracy is only seventy one percent, and steps average four point two against a minimum of three.

26
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:40.633
After tightening two tool descriptions, accuracy is eighty seven percent and steps drop to three point four, with the same completion.

27
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:42.581
Let's recap.

28
00:01:42.581 --> 00:01:45.200
Grade the trajectory, not just the answer.

29
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.767
Track tool accuracy, step efficiency, and completion.

30
00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.633
Hand label first, then calibrate a judge.

31
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:56.567
And run the suite on every change, so it feels better becomes three numbers moving.
